DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
The Revisionism Debate (Revisionismusstreit) of 1896--1899 constitutes one of
the most philosophically consequential controversies in the history of Marxism.
Eduard Bernstein's Evolutionary Socialism (1899) launched a systematic
critique of the theoretical pillars of Social Democratic orthodoxy: the
Marxian theory of capitalist breakdown (Zusammenbruchstheorie), dialectical
materialism, and the prediction of intensifying class polarisation. In
response, Kautsky published Bernstein und das sozialdemokratische Programm
(1899), defending the scientific credentials of historical materialism and
the necessity of a transformative rupture with capitalism. The debate was
not merely political but deeply philosophical: at stake were rival
conceptions of historical tendency, the relationship between empirical
evidence and theoretical necessity, the proper role of ethics in socialist
theory, and the meaning of 'scientific' social analysis.

The literature divides along several axes. Steger's intellectual biography of
Bernstein (1997) reconstructs how neo-Kantian ethics and British Fabian
gradualism reshaped Bernstein's philosophy of history from a necessity-driven
materialism to an open-ended, value-guided evolutionism. Gronow's analysis
(2016) places the dispute within the framework of Kautsky's distinctive
theory of capitalism, showing that Kautsky's 'orthodoxy' was itself a creative
synthesis of Darwinian naturalism, Spencerian evolutionism, and Marxian
political economy -- not simple dogmatism. The Melrose (2016) article on
the Revisionismusstreit illuminates the identity-political and knowledge-
authority dimensions of the controversy, while van Ree (2012) traces how the
debate over parliamentary versus revolutionary strategy reflected deeper
disputes about the historical trajectory of capitalism.

Luxemburg's Social Reform or Revolution (1900) remains the most philosophically
pointed response to Bernstein: she argued that the debate turned on whether
historical tendencies were mere empirical trends (revisable in light of new
data) or structural necessities grounded in the logic of capital. The
empirical test of the debate's economic claims has been extended into recent
scholarship (Bartels, Kersting, Wolf 2025), which uses Prussian regional data
to evaluate both sides' predictions about capital concentration and
immiseration.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
The revisionism debate is central to understanding Kautsky's philosophy of
history because it forced him to articulate and defend his philosophical
commitments explicitly. His response to Bernstein reveals the structure of
his historiosophy -- the role of scientific law, evolutionary tendency,
class agency, and revolutionary rupture -- in a way that his more systematic
works like Die materialistische Geschichtsauffassung do not, because there
he is less pressed by an adversary. The contrast between Kautsky's 'orthodox'
determinism and Bernstein's ethical gradualism is precisely where the
philosophical stakes of Kautsky's philosophy of history become most visible.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
There is relatively little sustained philosophical analysis (as opposed to
historical or political analysis) of how the Zusammenbruchstheorie functions
as a philosophy-of-history claim rather than as an economic prediction; the
modal status of historical tendencies in Kautsky vs. Bernstein deserves more
attention. The reception of the debate in non-German contexts (Austria,
Russia) is better covered in the political-historical literature than in
philosophy of history proper.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
The revisionism debate should be treated as a direct lens onto the
philosophical fault-lines in Kautsky's historical thinking: where his
commitment to historical necessity meets its critics. Links to Domain 1
(historical materialism) and Domain 3 (evolutionism and naturalism) are
especially strong.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Orthodox Kautskyian materialism: 4 papers -- Historical necessity, scientific
  socialism, breakdown of capitalism as structural tendency
- Revisionist/Bersteinian gradualism: 3 papers -- Neo-Kantian ethics, empirical
  fallibilism, evolutionary reform within capitalism
- Post-revisionist historiography: 4 papers -- Reception, identity politics,
  empirical reassessment, reception in Russia
====================================================================
}

@book{steger1997quest,
  author    = {Steger, Manfred B.},
  title     = {The Quest for Evolutionary Socialism: Eduard Bernstein and Social Democracy},
  year      = {1997},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  doi       = {10.1017/cbo9780511558603},
