@book{tudor1988marxism,
  author    = {Tudor, H. and Tudor, J. M.},
  title     = {Marxism and Social Democracy: The Revisionist Debate, 1896--1898},
  year      = {1988},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Tudor and Tudor's edited volume collects and translates into
  English the primary texts of the revisionism debate, including Bernstein's
  foundational articles in Die Neue Zeit (1896--1898) and responses from Kautsky,
  Luxemburg, and others. The volume provides the documentary basis for
  understanding the revisionist controversy as a philosophical exchange about
  historical materialism, the empirical status of Marx's predictions, and the
  relationship between theory and practice.

  RELEVANCE: Indispensable primary source collection for any study of the
  philosophical-historical dimension of the Bernstein--Kautsky debate. The
  texts collected here -- especially Bernstein's 'Problems of Socialism'
  articles and Kautsky's responses -- show the dispute unfolding as a debate
  about philosophy of history before it crystallised into the Evolutionary
  Socialism/Anti-Bernstein opposition.

  POSITION: Orthodox Kautskyian materialism and revisionism -- primary sources
  for the debate.
  },
  keywords = {Bernstein, Kautsky, revisionism, primary-source, Second-International, High},
  abstract = {Philip Kain’sMarx, Revolution and Social Democracypresents the case for a social-democratic reading of Marx. The central intention of the book is to convince the reader that market socialism is consistent with a democratic interpretation of Marx. Furthermore, Kain argues, a parliamentary democratic Marx requires neither a specifically socialist state nor public ownership of the means of production. Kain criticizes the Leninist vision of political revolution as a punctual transformation, resulting in state socialism, nationalized economies, centralized planning and so forth, as an authoritarian misrepresentation of Marx (66–75). Instead, Kain proposes, socialism—now understood as democratic planning that frames market regulation, welfare measures, majoritarian democracy and social rights—has been advancing throughout the twentieth century. He argues for seeing socialism as the result of a democratically-instigated, majority-led process of socialization rather than a political convulsion (79–80). Kain then reassures readers that this makes it possible to grasp what looked to Leninists like meliorism as the historical transformation of capitalism into socialism. Against the Leninist vision, Kain proposes market regulation and social measures controlled by a social-democratic parliamentary majority, which aim at reducing inequality and eliminating alienation. Rudolf Meidner’s plan for Swedish Social Democracy from the 1970s is presented in this context as an example of the social democratic transition to market socialism (132–135).
  abstract_source = {ndpr},

Tonally speaking, the book seems aimed at reassuring readerships interested in Marx as a philosophical figure, but wary of socialism because of the failure of historical communism. Despite strong engagement with both Marx and Engels, the book is not primarily a scholarly intervention in the Marxological field. Too many important debates—the connection between alienation, exploitation and domination; the definition of modes of production in terms of economic exploitation; participatory democracy versus parliamentary representation; and the notion of the elimination of the commodity form through collective ownership of the means of production, for instance—are dealt with through selective quotation, rather than detailed evaluation of the contending interpretations. Nor is there a searching critique of the historical results of actually existing social democracy, such as might be expected in countries where such traditions have held office for prolonged periods. Instead, the book addresses pro-socialist neophytes with the case for seeing social democratic politics as representing the authentic Marxian impulse. It promises them that this rules out the authoritarian communism that has strongly discredited the socialist project. Much of its argument is conducted in non-technical language, appropriate to making contact with philosophical and political novices.

As a rehabilitation of the very idea of taking Marx seriously, for an addressee who may be assumed to be mainly located in North America, the book does a very good job. However, there are some points where it might have done a better job by straightforwardly disagreeing with Marx, rather than prematurely smoothing out difficulties with the social democratic position in the interests of claiming the authority of the master thinker. After all, Marx is on the side of socialists and workers, not the other way around. And socialists today deserve the opportunity to consider Marx’s views in all of their complexity, irrespective of whether they agree with Lenin or Karl Kautsky. Indeed, it is perfectly possible that some ideas, such as the “withering away of the state” and distribution of abundance based on need, are orthogonal to reality. Market socialism and democratic parliamentarism may, or may not, be excellent answers to shortcomings of Marx’s nineteenth-century anticipation of twentieth-century experiments. But to find these rejoinders in the text without an extended discussion of why very serious interpreters have found just the opposite can sometimes seem unnecessarily apologetic, or proselytizing. This is especially the case when the position in question—similar in many respects to those of Kautsky—has been debated in considerable detail, in the context of historical efforts at political implementation.

The most contentious position—because systematically foundational to the entire interpretation—concerns the nature of alienation and its links to exploitation. According to Kain, the foundation for Marx’s critique of capitalism is his interpretation of alienation through commodity fetishism. He proposes that the whole point of socialism is to end alienation, grasped under capitalism as commodity fetishism, and the question would be, whether or not ending alienation is compatible with the persistence of markets. Kain builds up his answer in steps. First, he cites Marx to the effect that in commodity exchange, “a social relation of production appears as something existing apart from human beings, and the distinctive [social] relations. . .as properties of things” (19–20). He glosses this as the idea that:

If workers are alienated from their product, if it is lost to them, if it takes on a life of its own, if it becomes a power over them, it will come to dominate them. This is fetishism. (22)

Here, Kain notices that the “loss” in question happens because the capitalist appropriates the product legally by virtue of property arrangements. This means that alienation and exploitation are the same thing from two different perspectives (22). The clear implication is that ending alienation requires abolishing exploitation, which would have to involve the elimination of private property in the means of production (i.e., the property arrangements that cause commodity fetishism). This is not, however, the conclusion that Kain draws.

Instead, Kain argues that alienation can be eliminated while retaining private property in the means of production. This argument depends on an elision that the non-technical use of the term “market” seems to permit. In popular discourse, no distinction is made between “market,” meaning monetized commodity exchange between independent owners, and “market,” meaning the “market society,” a society characterised by private property in the means of production under conditions of capital accumulation. There can be markets (monetized exchange) without capital accumulation—this point is made in theGrundrisse,for instance. But it does not follow from this that Marxists are indifferent to the question of the property foundation on which market exchange happens. Kain tends to be ambiguous about this point. He references, but does not cite, Marx inCapital, who claims that fetishism-free social production might involve:

